# SHIFT 4 items (Tuesday 11/03 1900 to Wednesday 11/04 0700, night shift, E. Haddad RN). Exec'd by author.py so it can use E and parts.
items4 = []

items4.append({'id': '4.1', 'type': 'vitals', 'points': 1.0, 'title': 'The Peak of the Sepsis',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs; Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 1950)',
               'prompt': 'Set the clock to 1930 on Tuesday. Copy the vital signs at each time, circle every abnormal value, then answer below: (a) Calculate the mean arterial pressure at 1930 from the blood pressure and compare it with the MAP on the monitor. (b) Why is a MAP of about 69 NOT reassuring in Marcus at 1930?',
               'times': ['2026-11-03 19:30', '2026-11-03 23:00', '2026-11-04 03:00', '2026-11-04 06:00'],
               'times_label': ['Tue 1930', 'Tue 2300', 'Wed 0300', 'Wed 0600'],
               'rowsLabels': ['Temperature', 'Heart rate', 'Blood pressure', 'Respiratory rate', 'SpO2 (%)'],
               'codes': ['TEMP', 'HR', 'BP', 'RR', 'SPO2'],
               'key': {'rules': 'Cells 0.75 x (cells correct / 20), rounded to the nearest 0.25; the two answers are 0.25 together (0.125 each). Allow +/- 2 for rates and +/- 0.2 F. Values: 1930: T 102.4, HR 133, BP 107/50, RR 30, SpO2 92; 2300: T 101.4, HR 117, BP 111/55, RR 26, SpO2 96; 0300: T 100.5, HR 108, BP 111/60, RR 20, SpO2 98; 0600: T 100.2, HR 101, BP 110/59, RR 19, SpO2 97. (a) MAP = (107 + 2 x 50) / 3 = 69 mmHg, which matches the monitor (69). (b) The MAP of 69 is only being kept above the goal of 65 by norepinephrine (0.10 at 1900, 0.12 at 1930, 0.14 from 2030), with fever 102.4, HR 133, RR 30 and lactate 4.1: he is in septic shock and the pressure would fall without the drug. The diastolic of 50 shows vasodilation.'}})
items4.append({'id': '4.1b', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0, 'lines': 6, 'title': 'MAP calculation and why 69 is not reassuring (scored with 4.1)', 'key': {'answer': 'Scored with item 4.1.'}})

items4.append({'id': '4.2', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.5, 'lines': 8, 'title': 'The 1845 Blood Gas',
               'prompt': 'Open Lab Results (Blood Gas) and the ventilator settings for 1845. (a) Interpret the ABG in one sentence (acid-base disorder and what the body is doing about it) and name the lab drawn at 1900 that explains it. (b) Calculate the PaO2/FiO2 ratio and say what the chest x-ray at 1840 adds.',
               'where': 'Lab Results (Blood Gas, Lactate); Chart Review > Notes (Dr. Brandt 1755, Nursing Note 1850 for the ventilator change); Chart Review > Imaging (Chest X-Ray 1840)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('pH 7.31, HCO3 18, base excess -8 = metabolic acidosis (PaCO2 36 is low-normal because he is breathing at 30 per minute: respiratory compensation, not a respiratory problem). Explained by the lactate of 4.1 at 1900 (lactic acidosis from shock); mafenide soaks can also add to a metabolic acidosis.', 0.25, 'ABG 1845, lactate 1900'),
                   E('PaO2 92 / FiO2 0.50 = 184 (accept 180-190): a P/F under 200 on PEEP 8 is in the moderate ARDS range; the x-ray shows new bilateral perihilar/interstitial opacities, no consolidation = early ARDS physiology (capillary leak of sepsis), which is why the vent was changed to PEEP 8 and FiO2 0.50 at 1800 (set RR 18).', 0.25, 'Vent, CXR')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.3', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.0, 'lines': 11, 'title': 'Norepinephrine: Rate and Safety',
               'prompt': 'Open the MAR and the Orders. Norepinephrine 16 mcg/mL was 0.10 mcg/kg/min at 1900, 0.12 at 1930 and 0.14 at 2030. His dosing weight is 82 kg. (a) Calculate the pump rate in mL/h at 0.14 mcg/kg/min. (b) What MAP are you titrating to, and why is the arterial line and a central lumen used? (c) Give two safety checks for a vasopressor infusion.',
               'where': 'MAR (norepinephrine); Orders; Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 1755, Dr. Brandt 1755)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('0.14 mcg/kg/min x 82 kg = 11.48 mcg/min x 60 = 688.8 mcg/h; / 16 mcg/mL = 43 mL/h (accept 43.0-43.1).', 0.5, 'MAR'),
                   E('MAP 65 mmHg or higher (order: titrate by 0.02 mcg/kg/min every 5 minutes; the notes allow 0.02-0.04 every 5-10 minutes; accept either). A-line gives beat-to-beat pressure while the dose is changing; vasopressors are given through a central, dedicated lumen because they cause severe vasoconstriction and tissue injury if they leak into the skin.', 0.25, 'Note 1755'),
                   E('Any two: independent double check of the concentration and rate; do not flush or bolus the line; hourly site check of the left IJ (swelling, blanching) and stop/notify per protocol if infiltration is suspected; level and zero the transducer; never stop it abruptly; keep a back-up ready; titrate only per the order.', 0.25, '')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.4', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.0, 'lines': 16, 'title': 'Hydrocortisone, Glucose and the Insulin Infusion',
               'prompt': 'Open the MAR, the Orders, Lab Results (point-of-care glucose and potassium) and the pharmacy note of 1930. (a) Hydrocortisone 50 mg IV q6h started at 2030: why would Dr. Brandt order a stress dose, and how many doses did you give this shift? (b) List the glucose values at 1930, 2130 and 0230 and the insulin rate changes between 1900 and 0300, and explain why the glucose rose after the steroid. (c) Why were the insulin rates lowered, and what is the hypoglycemia risk in Marcus? (d) Which electrolyte do you watch with insulin, and what were his values?',
               'where': 'MAR (hydrocortisone, insulin infusion); Lab Results (Glucose, Potassium); Chart Review > Notes (Pharmacy 1930, Dr. Brandt 2345)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Septic shock that still needs norepinephrine at 0.1 or higher (0.14 at 2030): critical illness can cause a relative adrenal insufficiency; hydrocortisone helps the vessels respond to norepinephrine and shortens time on the pressor. Two doses given by the night nurse: 2030 and 0230 (next 0830); the order is 50 mg q6h for 3 doses (third due 0830), then 25 mg q6h until the ICU team stops it; pharmacy plans a taper once off pressors, so never stop it abruptly.', 0.5, 'MAR, pharmacy note'),
                   E('Glucose 246 (1930), 251 (2030), 266 (2130, peak), then 257, 241, 225, 212, 197 (0230). Insulin 4 units/h (running since 1800), raised to 5 at 2100, 3 at 0000, 1.5 at 0300. Hydrocortisone (a glucocorticoid) and sepsis stress hormones cause insulin resistance and more glucose release from the liver; the glucose was already rising with the sepsis (227 at 1730) and the peak came about one hour after the first dose.', 0.5, 'Labs, MAR'),
                   E('Rates are lowered as the glucose falls toward the goal 140-180 mg/dL and as the sepsis improves (less insulin resistance). Risk of hypoglycemia: insulin requirement falls quickly while the feeding is constant at 70 mL/h; if the tube feeding is held or stops (OR, residual, tube problem) the glucose can drop fast; check hourly and after every rate change; per the order: glucose under 100 or tube feeding held = hold the infusion and call the provider; glucose under 70 = notify and give D50 per protocol, recheck.', 0.5, 'Order'),
                   E('Potassium: insulin moves potassium into the cells (hypokalemia). K 3.8 (1700), 4.0 (2300), 4.1 (0500): acceptable, but keep 4.0 or higher, replace per protocol, and check with each BMP.', 0.5, 'Labs')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.5', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.0, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'The 2100 Labs and the 2130 Gram Stain',
               'prompt': 'Open the CBC at 2100 (compare with 1555 and with admission) and the wound Gram stain at 2130. (a) What do a WBC of 21.8 with 14% bands tell you? (b) Platelets fell from 171 to 142 (and were 288 on admission) while he receives heparin 5,000 units SQ: what percent fall from admission is that, and what do you ask or check before you decide to hold the heparin? (c) What does "Gram-positive cocci in clusters" most likely mean, and do the antibiotics already cover it? (d) What does contact precautions mean for you today?',
               'where': 'Lab Results (CBC with differential, Microbiology); Chart Review > Notes (Result Notification 2140); Orders (Precautions)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('WBC 21.8 (up from 18.4) with 14% bands (immature neutrophils, a "left shift") = the bone marrow is being driven hard by a serious bacterial infection; consistent with burn wound sepsis (segs 68%).', 0.25, 'CBC 2100'),
                   E('(288 - 142) / 288 = 51% fall since admission (already 128 at 0500 = 56%). Pharmacy asks to assess for HIT when platelets fall more than 50%: do not simply hold or continue; report the number and ask the provider; Dr. Brandt judged HIT unlikely (timing under 5 days of heparin, sepsis explains it). Watch for bleeding, trend platelets with the next CBC, and look at INR/fibrinogen for DIC (INR 1.3, fibrinogen 350 at 0500).', 0.25, 'Platelets, pharmacy note'),
                   E('Gram-positive cocci in clusters = Staphylococcus, most likely S. aureus (suspected MSSA; MRSA not excluded until the culture finishes); a Gram stain is preliminary and does not give the antibiotic susceptibility. Vancomycin plus pip-tazo already cover it, so no change was ordered; wait for the final culture and sensitivities. The lab called the result at 2130 and the nurse called Dr. Brandt with read-back; Dr. Okafor to be told.', 0.25, 'Gram stain 2130, note 2140'),
                   E('Contact precautions (burn unit): single room, gown and gloves for room entry and all wound care (plus mask for dressing changes), dedicated equipment (stethoscope, cuff), hand hygiene before and after, clean/dirty zones, sign on the door; accept equivalent items (the order says gown and gloves for all wound care and room entry, dedicated equipment). S. aureus spreads by hands and equipment.', 0.25, 'Orders')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.6', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.75, 'lines': 11, 'title': 'Fever and Cooling',
               'prompt': 'Open the temperature trend and the MAR (acetaminophen) from 1900 to 0600. (a) Write the temperature at 1930, 2200, 0000, 0300 and 0600 and the times acetaminophen 1 g IV was given. (b) Why record the temperature before you give acetaminophen, and why can you not give all the credit for the falling fever to acetaminophen? (c) Name two cooling measures you used or could use in a burn patient, and one thing you avoid.',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs (Temperature, bladder); MAR (acetaminophen); Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 1950); Lab Results (AST, ALT)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('T 102.4 (1930), 101.6 (2200), 101.0 (0000), 100.5 (0300), 100.2 (0600). Acetaminophen 1,000 mg IV at 2200 and 0600 (scheduled q8h).', 0.25, 'Vitals, MAR'),
                   E('Acetaminophen lowers the temperature and can mask the true fever curve; record the temperature before the dose so the team sees the real trend. The fever was already falling before the 2200 dose (102.4 at 1930 -> 101.9 at 2100) and kept falling as the antibiotics, steroids and source control worked, so the fall is partly acetaminophen and partly a real response. (AST 96/ALT 84 at 0500: keep to 3 g/day.)', 0.25, ''),
                   E('Any two: remove extra linens, lower room temperature, cool cloths to axillae/groin away from burns, fan; core temperature monitoring (bladder probe every 30 minutes). Avoid: ice baths or cold water on the burn wounds (heat loss and hypothermia) and shivering, which raises oxygen use and heart rate.', 0.25, 'Note 1950')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.7', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.5, 'lines': 8, 'title': 'Mafenide Soaks',
               'prompt': 'The right arm and chest are treated with mafenide 5% soaks every 8 hours (1730, 0130). (a) Why is the pain different with this agent, and how do you score it in Marcus? (b) Mafenide can cause an acid-base problem: which one, and which lab results from this night would you check?',
               'where': 'MAR (mafenide, hydromorphone); Orders; Flowsheets (CPOT); Lab Results (Blood Gas, BMP)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Mafenide penetrates eschar but causes burning pain on application; premedicate with the ordered analgesic 15 minutes before the soak (order: hydromorphone 15 minutes before dressing changes; the MAR shows 0.5 mg at 1915 for wound care discomfort, and none is charted before the 0130 soak, so a good answer notes the 0130 soak should be premedicated) and score pain with the CPOT because he is intubated and cannot report pain (CPOT 1/8 hourly after 1930).', 0.25, 'MAR, CPOT'),
                   E('It inhibits carbonic anhydrase and can cause a metabolic acidosis (hyperchloremic): check ABG pH/HCO3 and the BMP bicarbonate and chloride (HCO3 18 at 1845, 20 at 2230, 22 at 0400; CO2 22, 21, 22 and chloride 107, 106, 106 on the BMPs, so no worsening acidosis); report a falling bicarbonate or rising chloride.', 0.25, 'Labs')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.8', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.5, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'Sedation and Delirium Risk',
               'prompt': 'Open the Orders (RASS goal), the flowsheet and the MAR. (a) What is the RASS goal for Tuesday, what was his RASS (charted 2000, 0000 and 0400) on propofol 40 mcg/kg/min and fentanyl 150 mcg/h, and why did Dr. Brandt accept the deeper sedation tonight? (b) Name two delirium risk factors he has and two things you do about them.',
               'where': 'Orders (Sedation and pain scales); Flowsheets (RASS, CAM-ICU, Respiratory); MAR (propofol, fentanyl); Chart Review > Notes (Dr. Brandt 2345)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Goal -1 to -2 on Tuesday; his RASS was -3 at 2000 and 0000 (deeper than the goal) because he was triggering the ventilator (RR 30) in septic shock with early ARDS (PEEP 8, FiO2 0.50) and needed comfort and synchrony; no SAT tonight (Dr. Brandt). When he improved, propofol was lowered to 30 and fentanyl to 100 at 0400 and RASS became -2.', 0.25, 'Order, vitals, note'),
                   E('Any two risks: sepsis, deep sedation/fentanyl, mechanical ventilation, steroids, sleep loss, restraint, alcohol use (withdrawal window 48-96 h is Wed-Thu); actions: CAM-ICU every shift (negative at 2000, 0000, 0400), lowest effective sedation and analgesia first, lights/noise down at night, cluster care, reorient and talk to him, family at the bedside, avoid benzodiazepines, remove restraints when not needed.', 0.25, 'CAM-ICU')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.9', 'type': 'choice', 'points': 1.0, 'style': 'multi', 'title': 'Septic Shock That Is Not Responding',
               'stem': 'HYPOTHETICAL (not in the chart): at 2100 the monitor shows MAP 58 mmHg, HR 134 and urine output 12 mL/h for 2 hours while the norepinephrine is running at 0.14 mcg/kg/min through the left IJ central line. Which nursing actions are appropriate for Marcus?',
               'prompt': 'Choose all that apply.',
               'where': 'Think through the sepsis bundle, MAR (norepinephrine, hydrocortisone) and Orders; this scenario is not in the chart.',
               'options': ['Notify Dr. Brandt now with SBAR: MAP under 65 on a rising dose of norepinephrine.',
                           'Check the arterial line (level, zero, compare with a cuff pressure) and confirm the pump rate, concentration and lumen.',
                           'Turn the norepinephrine down for a few minutes to see whether his pressure recovers on its own.',
                           'Move the norepinephrine to the left antecubital peripheral IV so the central line can be used for fluid.',
                           'Expect orders for a second vasopressor (for example vasopressin) and give any ordered fluid in measured boluses, listening to the lungs and watching the ventilator pressures after each one.',
                           'Give lactated Ringer\'s wide open until the MAP is above 65, because burn patients always need more fluid.',
                           'Hold the next hydrocortisone and antibiotic doses until the blood pressure improves.',
                           'Look for other causes of a falling pressure: bleeding, a tense abdomen (bladder pressure over 15 mmHg), a pneumothorax on the ventilator and the effect of propofol and fentanyl.',
                           'Recheck the lactate and glucose, keep hourly urine output and neurovascular checks, and document the times and the notifications.'],
               'key': {'answer': ['Notify Dr. Brandt now with SBAR...',
                                  'Check the arterial line (level, zero, compare with a cuff pressure)...',
                                  'Expect orders for a second vasopressor... measured boluses...',
                                  'Look for other causes of a falling pressure...',
                                  'Recheck the lactate and glucose, keep hourly urine output...'],
                       'rules': '0.2 per correct option selected; subtract 0.2 for each incorrect option selected (not below 0). Wrong options: turning the pressor down (it is the treatment, never reduce or stop it without an order); a peripheral IV for the main vasopressor (central lumen is the standard; only short term with protocol); unlimited fluid (burn patients and the early ARDS lungs, PEEP 8, edema 2-3+ and weight gain make fluid creep dangerous); holding the steroid and antibiotics (both are part of the shock treatment and are given on time).'}})

items4.append({'id': '4.10', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.0, 'lines': 16, 'title': 'The Critical Result at 0240',
               'prompt': 'Open Lab Results (Microbiology) and the nursing note of 0300. At 0240 the microbiology lab calls you. (a) What do you do during the call (what do you say and write down)? (b) Who do you notify, and in what time frame? (c) Write the SBAR you give with the real values. (d) Which line is involved, and what do you assess at the bedside?',
               'where': 'Lab Results (Blood culture 0240); Chart Review > Notes (Critical Result Call 0300, Pharmacy 1930); Flowsheets; Orders (central line care)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Write the result down (or enter it) with the caller\'s name and the time, read it back (patient name and second identifier, blood culture set 2 from the central line, bottle 1 of 4 positive at 10 hours, Gram-positive cocci in clusters) and get the caller to verify it ("read back verified"); document the time of the call (0240).', 0.5, 'Note 0300'),
                   E('Dr. Brandt (intensivist) was called at 0245; also tell the charge nurse and make sure Dr. Okafor (burn surgeon) and pharmacy (A. Chen) are aware; critical results go to a provider immediately (within the policy time, usually 15-30 minutes at most) and the response and any order are documented.', 0.5, 'Note 0300'),
                   E('S: critical blood culture, 1 of 4 bottles, central line set, GPC in clusters. B: burn wound sepsis, cultures drawn 1550 before antibiotics, vancomycin and pip-tazo since the evening, wound Gram stain also GPC in clusters at 2130, left IJ line day 3. A: about 0200-0300 T 100.5-100.7, HR 107-108, BP 109-111/55-60 (MAP 73-77), lactate 1.8 (0200), norepinephrine 0.08, improving; line site clean. R: ask whether to continue vancomycin and pip-tazo (next vancomycin 0430), when to repeat blood cultures, and who decides about the line once the organism is identified (ID consult).', 0.5, ''),
                   E('Left IJ triple lumen (placed Monday 0345): dressing dry, intact and dated; look and feel for erythema, drainage, tenderness and neck swelling; flush each lumen and check blood return; hub scrub 15 seconds; review whether it is still needed (norepinephrine and antibiotics): it stays until the team decides; do not remove it on your own. One positive bottle can be a contaminant, but with a matching wound Gram stain and S. aureus likely, treat it as real until the identification returns.', 0.5, 'Note 0300')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.11', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.0, 'lines': 17, 'title': 'Reading the Pressor Graph',
               'prompt': 'Study the graph of MAP, norepinephrine dose and lactate for this shift. (a) What happened to the MAP while the dose was raised from 0.10 to 0.14 and then lowered to zero? (b) How did the lactate change, and what does that mean? (c) What did you need to see before it was safe to wean, and who sets the criteria? (d) After the pressor is off, what do you watch, and what would make you call the provider?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs (MAP); MAR (norepinephrine); Lab Results (Lactate); Chart Review > Notes (Dr. Brandt 2345, Nursing Note 0450)',
               'images': [{'path': 'figs/fig_s4_pressor.png', 'caption': 'Figure: MAP, norepinephrine dose and lactate, Tuesday 1900 to Wednesday 0700.', 'width': 6.2}],
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('From 1900 to about 2130 the MAP stayed flat at 69-71 even though the dose was raised 0.10 -> 0.12 (1930) -> 0.14 (2030): more drug was needed just to hold above 65 (worst of the sepsis). By 2300-0000 the MAP climbed to 73-75 on the same 0.14 (antibiotics, steroid and albumin working). From 0000 the dose was stepped down (0.12, 0.08 at 0200, 0.04 at 0400, off 0430) and the MAP stayed 73-78, never below 65: no fall off the pressor.', 0.5, 'Graph'),
                   E('Lactate 4.1 (1900) -> 2.6 (2230) -> 1.8 (0200) -> 1.5 (0500): a fall of about 37% in 3.5 hours ((4.1 - 2.6) / 4.1), normal (under 2) by 0200. A falling lactate means the tissues are getting enough blood flow and oxygen (less anaerobic metabolism); a rising lactate would mean worse shock.', 0.5, 'Labs'),
                   E('MAP at or above goal (65, here 73-77) on a stable, low dose; lactate falling or normal; urine output at least 0.5 mL/kg/h (41 mL/h for 82 kg; he made 51-68 mL/h); warm skin, brisk capillary refill, heart rate coming down (133 to 108), no new fluid bolus, infection treated. The criteria come from the provider: the order says wean by 0.02 mcg/kg/min every 15-30 minutes when MAP is over 75, and Dr. Brandt\'s plan (2345) says once MAP is 70 or higher and lactate under 2. The first step down at 0000 was made with MAP 74-75 and lactate 2.6 (falling; 1.8 at 0200); accept any answer that names a MAP above goal with a falling lactate. The nurse weans only per the provider\'s order, one small step at a time, and stops and calls if the MAP drops under 65.', 0.5, 'Note 2345'),
                   E('Recheck MAP every 15 minutes for the first hour, then hourly (done after 0430: MAP 77, 75, 76); heart rate, urine output, capillary refill/skin temperature, mental status, temperature and the next lactate; hydrocortisone continues. Call Dr. Brandt for MAP under 65, a rising heart rate, falling urine output, cool mottled skin or a rising lactate: the pressor may need to restart; keep the central line and the drug available.', 0.5, 'Note 0450')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.12', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.0, 'lines': 12, 'title': 'The Kidneys, the Vancomycin Level and Procalcitonin',
               'prompt': 'Open Lab Results (Creatinine, Vancomycin level, Procalcitonin) and the pharmacy note. (a) Write the creatinine at 1700, 2300 and 0500 and say what change this is (and whether his urine output agrees). (b) Why does the combination of vancomycin and piperacillin-tazobactam worry the pharmacist? (c) The vancomycin level of 11.6 was drawn at 0415: why is it drawn before the 0430 dose, and what question do you ask? (d) What does procalcitonin 9.6 add?',
               'where': 'Lab Results (Chemistry, Drug levels, Procalcitonin); Chart Review > Notes (Pharmacy 1930, Nursing Notes 0300 and 0450); Intake/Output',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Creatinine 1.1 (1700) -> 1.3 (2300) -> 1.4 (0500; baseline about 1.0): a rise of 0.3 mg/dL in 12 hours = acute kidney injury, stage 1 (KDIGO); BUN 27 -> 30. Urine output actually improved (0.34 mL/kg/h at 1900-2000 to 0.79 at 0500-0600), so it is a non-oliguric injury; likely sepsis/hypoperfusion plus nephrotoxic drugs.', 0.25, 'Labs'),
                   E('Vancomycin plus pip-tazo is associated with more AKI than either drug alone; both depend on the kidneys, so a falling clearance makes levels rise; pharmacy will reassess vancomycin when the culture returns (if MSSA, a beta-lactam).', 0.25, 'Pharmacy note'),
                   E('Drugs with a narrow safe range are checked by levels; the level is drawn at 0415, 15 minutes before the dose, so it shows the lowest concentration and clearance (11.6; pharmacy uses AUC dosing, not a trough alone). Draw it on time, and ask pharmacy/provider to review the 0430 dose of 1,250 mg in light of the rising creatinine (1.3 at 2300) before or after it runs; avoid NSAIDs, contrast and other nephrotoxins; strict urine output.', 0.25, 'Pharmacy note'),
                   E('Procalcitonin 9.6 ng/mL (4.8 at 1555): high, a marker of severe bacterial infection and sepsis; it supports the diagnosis and the trend is used later to decide when to stop antibiotics; it does not identify the organism.', 0.25, 'Labs')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.13', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.75, 'lines': 11, 'title': 'The Night Shift Intake and Output',
               'prompt': 'Open the Intake/Output flowsheet (Totals) and the albumin order. (a) Write the totals for "Night shift 11/03 1900 to 11/04 0700" and the net balance, and (use the printed shift total, not your own sum of the hourly rows; ignore the later day-shift row) and compare the weight at 0400 (91.2 kg) with the admission weight (82 kg). (b) The output for 0200-0300 is 280 mL: how much is urine and what is the rest? (c) Albumin 5% 500 mL ran 2200 to 0200: what mL/h is that, why was it given, and what do you watch for?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Intake/Output (shift totals, hourly rows); Flowsheets (Daily weight, Bowel); MAR (albumin); Orders',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Intake 4,160 mL, output 803 mL, net +3,357 mL (4,160 - 803). Weight 91.2 kg vs 82 kg = +9.2 kg (about 11% above admission): fluid (edema 2-3+) from the capillary leak; watch lungs, ventilator pressures, edema, bladder pressure.', 0.25, 'I&O totals, weight'),
                   E('Only 60 mL of the 280 mL at 0200-0300 is urine; the rest (220 mL) is a loose stool (tube-feeding related), so do not call it a diuresis. Real urine output improved from 28 mL/h (1900-2000, 0.34 mL/kg/h) to 68 mL/h (0600-0700, 0.83 mL/kg/h).', 0.25, 'I&O hourly'),
                   E('500 mL / 4 h = 125 mL/h. Albumin 5% is a colloid: it draws and holds fluid in the vessels, supports the pressure and urine output in septic shock and burn leak with less crystalloid (LR was lowered to 75 mL/h at 0200). Watch for fluid overload: crackles, rising peak/plateau pressures, SpO2, edema, CVP, and for a BP rise.', 0.25, 'MAR, order')]}})

items4.append({'id': '4.14', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.0, 'lines': 18, 'title': 'Handoff at 0650',
               'prompt': 'It is 0650 on Wednesday. Write your SBAR handoff for J. Morales, RN, the day nurse. Use real numbers, the events of the night, what is due in the next hours (look at the MAR and Orders), and anything you want the day team to follow up.',
               'where': 'Everything you read and charted this night; Orders; MAR; Lab Results; Flowsheets',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Situation: Marcus Delaney, BICU-6, full code, NKDA, contact precautions; burn wound septic shock (MSSA suspected), now improving; off norepinephrine since 0430 (MAP 75-77); intubated.', 0.5, 'Note 0650'),
                   E('Background: 22% TBSA flame burn with inhalation injury, hospital day 3; cultures 1550 Tuesday before pip-tazo and vancomycin; hydrocortisone 50 mg q6h since 2030; wound Gram stain GPC in clusters (2130) and blood culture bottle positive GPC in clusters at 0240 (identification pending); left IJ line day 3, site clean.', 0.5, ''),
                   E('Assessment: T 100.2 (from 102.4), HR 101, BP 110/59 (MAP 76), RR 19, SpO2 97%; vent AC/VC 450, RR 16, PEEP 5, FiO2 0.40 since 0600; lactate 4.1 -> 1.5; WBC 19.2 (bands 9%), platelets 128, creatinine 1.4 (peak), PCT 9.6, vancomycin level 11.6; UOP 65 mL/h (0.79 mL/kg/h); glucose 175 on insulin 1.5 units/h; propofol 30, fentanyl 100, RASS -2, CPOT 1; I&O +3,357; weight 91.2 kg.', 0.5, ''),
                   E('Recommendation: MAP every hour and call for under 65; SAT/SBT at 0800; hydrocortisone and pip-tazo due 0830; mafenide soak due about 0930 with premedication; follow creatinine, urine output, platelets and glucose (hourly; hypoglycemia risk as insulin needs fall); pharmacy review of vancomycin; ID/burn team about the culture and the line; wean sedation/FiO2; acetaminophen next 1400 (LFTs); family updated, phone numbers on file.', 0.5, '')]}})

